@startuml fig09_state_request
!include theme.iuml
title Figure 9 — State Machine: Request Lifecycle

state "Generated" as GEN
state "Released" as REL
state screen <<choice>>
state "Excluded" as EXC <<terminal>>
state "Queued" as Q
state "Dispatched" as D
state "In Flight" as IF {
  state "Prefill" as PF
  state "Decode" as DEC
  [*] --> PF
  PF --> DEC : first token
  DEC --> [*] : output_tokens reached\n(ignore_eos forces exact length)
  DEC : joins and leaves an engine-owned\nrunning batch mid-generation
}
state "Timed Out" as TO
state "Failed" as F <<terminal>>
state "Completed" as CP
state "Recorded" as RC
state "Discarded (warmup)" as DIS <<terminal>>
state "Counted in p50 / p95 / p99" as CNT <<terminal>>

[*] --> GEN : gen_trace.py, offline, seeded
GEN --> REL : replay.py releases at\nscheduled offset (open-loop)
REL --> screen : scheduler admission check
screen --> EXC : outside admissible set (F-13)
screen --> Q : admissible on >= 1 node
EXC --> [*] : counted in the cliff characterisation\n(F-15), never in the primary tail statistics

Q --> D : policy.choose() returns node_id\nDecisionRecord written (F-3)
D --> IF : accepted by worker
IF --> CP : full response returned
IF --> TO : exceeds timeout ceiling
TO --> D : retry whole request\n(no partial state — streaming\nis excluded from the base system)
TO --> F : retry budget exhausted
CP --> RC
F --> RC
RC --> DIS : arrival inside warmup window
RC --> CNT : arrival inside measurement window

note right of Q
  queue_wait begins here and is
  measured entirely on one machine.
end note

note bottom of TO
  Under extreme R this is a categorical
  failure, not a latency tail. Admission
  is what keeps it rare enough that tail
  statistics stay meaningful.
end note
@enduml
